1
00:00:01,010 --> 00:00:03,310
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,570 --> 00:00:04,010
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:09,950
My name is Catherine and today we're going to be helping you set up an answering machine basically.

4
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:10,850
That's right.

5
00:00:11,110 --> 00:00:17,070
So somebody's calling you, you can't answer the phone so you leave a message and we're going to teach you all about that today.

6
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:17,690
That's right.

7
00:00:17,890 --> 00:00:22,210
So let's take a listen to our dialogue and we'll be back in a moment to figure out just what's going on.

8
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:30,880
Can you help me set up my voicemail message?

9
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:35,360
I just got this service and I'm not really sure what I'm supposed to say.

10
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:36,140
Sure.

11
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:42,460
You just basically got to let the caller know who they called and ask them for their contact information so you can call them back.

12
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:43,080
Okay.

13
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:45,820
So I can say, this is Abby's voicemail.

14
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:47,260
I will call you later.

15
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:48,780
So leave me your name and number.

16
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,260
That's more or less the idea, but try something that sounds more friendly.

17
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:53,400
Okay.

18
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:54,600
So how about this?

19
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:57,380
This is Abby and I'm really happy you called.

20
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:00,460
I promise I will give you a ring as soon as I can.

21
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:02,500
So please leave me your name and number.

22
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:03,740
Talk to you soon.

23
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:06,200
A little too friendly, Abby.

24
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:07,260
Just say this.

25
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:08,940
Hi, you've reached Abby.

26
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:14,120
I'm unable to answer your call right now, but if you leave me your name and phone number, I will get back to you as soon as possible.

27
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:14,700
Thanks.

28
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:15,940
That's perfect.

29
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:18,760
Can you say that again and record it for me?

30
00:01:22,540 --> 00:01:23,280
All right, we're back.

31
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:25,580
So now let's take a look at some language takeaway.

32
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:29,380
Language takeaway.

33
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:31,720
All right.

34
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:37,180
In the first sentence, we are asking someone to help me set up my voicemail message.

35
00:01:37,180 --> 00:01:39,460
So we got two key words there.

36
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:40,140
That's right.

37
00:01:40,180 --> 00:01:41,000
So let's break it up.

38
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:44,560
The first one is a phrasal verb, to set up.

39
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:45,560
Okay.

40
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:50,890
You can set a lot of things up, but just remember that set and up, these are the two words that go together.

41
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:52,480
In this case, we're setting up voicemail.

42
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:57,040
It means to establish or to start or to put together.

43
00:01:57,250 --> 00:01:57,910
Yeah, to install.

44
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:03,920
Like for example, you get a new computer and you have to connect all the cables and you have to connect the monitor.

45
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:06,120
So you say, can you help me set up my computer?

46
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:07,120
That's right.

47
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:08,070
So he always helps me set it up.

48
00:02:08,530 --> 00:02:10,090
So my computer, for example.

49
00:02:10,670 --> 00:02:10,910
That's right.

50
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:15,460
And what we're setting up is our voicemail message.

51
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:17,680
So what exactly is voicemail?

52
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:19,030
Well you know what mail is.

53
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:20,570
We have email and regular mail.

54
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:28,960
Voicemail is the kind of message you receive on your phone, your cell phone or your home phone when you don't answer the phone.

55
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:29,130
That's right.

56
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:34,510
So basically a way for people to leave you a message even though you can't answer them.

57
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:35,270
That's right.

58
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:38,910
So this is something that's, it's not text, it's actually a voice.

59
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:42,800
So that's why it's called voicemail and you have to set it up.

60
00:02:42,810 --> 00:02:42,990
Okay.

61
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:45,760
So there's, there's no automatic voicemail message.

62
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:48,390
You actually have to make your own or you can choose one.

63
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:49,670
And today we're making our own.

64
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:50,270
That's right.

65
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:58,120
And so we're explaining that we actually just got this service and we don't really know what we're supposed to say or how to set up our voicemail.

66
00:02:58,800 --> 00:02:59,800
That's right.

67
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:02,440
So voicemail messages are a kind of service.

68
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:05,140
And now who would offer this kind of service, Marco?

69
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:07,480
Probably your telephone company.

70
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:08,060
That's right.

71
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:15,420
So a telephone company will offer many different services and these are things that you can pay extra for and they're useful.

72
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:16,640
They can help you in some way.

73
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:19,800
So another kind of service is three-way calling.

74
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:20,720
Right.

75
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:21,300
That's right.

76
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:24,600
So actually we're taking a look at this word service as a noun.

77
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:27,660
So we have a service or this service.

78
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:30,980
So the company offers this service.

79
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:34,180
And well, why are we setting up the voicemail?

80
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:40,540
And our friend explains that we just basically doing it to let the caller know who they called.

81
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:41,880
Okay.

82
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:44,820
So if I call a number and it says, Hey, leave a message.

83
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:48,280
I don't know who I'm leaving a message for.

84
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:50,040
Did I dial the wrong number?

85
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:51,800
What do I say?

86
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:52,220
Do I say my phone number?

87
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:55,300
So this is basically instructions for what to say.

88
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:55,880
That's right.

89
00:03:56,760 --> 00:03:57,020
And you become the caller.

90
00:03:57,760 --> 00:03:58,760
Right.

91
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:01,660
So whoever is making the call is called the caller.

92
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:08,320
And this is why we are setting up our voicemail to let the caller know who we are calling.

93
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:13,620
And of course we have to ask the caller for their contact information.

94
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:14,920
All right.

95
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:20,160
Now this is important because it's not just something you have with voicemail messages.

96
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:22,360
You have contact information for many different things.

97
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:26,580
For example, your medical care, your school, you have to include contact information.

98
00:04:27,220 --> 00:04:30,940
Now this would be your phone number, your address and or your email.

99
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:31,740
That's right.

100
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:44,180
So your contact information is not only useful for in a voicemail message, but for example, in a business meeting or a business lunch, you want to get maybe someone else's contact information so you can call them later.

101
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:44,740
That's right.

102
00:04:44,860 --> 00:04:46,540
So don't forget, it's also your name as well.

103
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:48,560
So I forgot to mention that.

104
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:48,700
That's right.

105
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:50,520
Contact information must have your name.

106
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:51,540
All right.

107
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:55,900
So that's pretty much all we have for the first part of language takeaway.

108
00:04:56,440 --> 00:04:59,980
Why don't we listen to the dialogue again and we'll be back in a bit with Fluency Builder.

109
