But if you leave me your name and phone number, I will get back to you as soon as possible.

251
00:11:13,320 --> 00:11:13,520
Thanks.

252
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:14,760
That's perfect.

253
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:17,600
Can you say that again and record it for me?

254
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:20,440
All right.

255
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:29,280
So talking about voicemail, I think in the United States, this is a very, very popular and almost necessary service.

256
00:11:29,740 --> 00:11:30,220
That's right.

257
00:11:30,540 --> 00:11:38,620
So in America, if you buy a phone plan, which most people have, that means that you can use your phone in a certain way.

258
00:11:38,840 --> 00:11:44,360
Like every day you have free nights or you have a thousand minutes per month to talk.

259
00:11:44,960 --> 00:11:47,680
But you sometimes also get these services or you pay extra for them.

260
00:11:48,280 --> 00:11:53,920
And voicemail is very important because people don't text as much in America as they do in other countries.

261
00:11:54,520 --> 00:12:00,120
So I know in other places, sometimes you text a message to someone and they receive it and okay, it's a message.

262
00:12:00,420 --> 00:12:03,000
But if you don't do that, you actually have to record a message.

263
00:12:03,680 --> 00:12:06,280
Like when I call my parents, they never answer the phone.

264
00:12:06,280 --> 00:12:08,920
So I always have to leave a voicemail message.

265
00:12:09,120 --> 00:12:10,960
So I say, hi, it's Catherine.

266
00:12:10,960 --> 00:12:11,180
It's Saturday.

267
00:12:11,960 --> 00:12:12,300
Where are you?

268
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:13,960
Call me.

269
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:14,960
Right.

270
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:15,960
Okay.

271
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:20,880
So that's that kind of, I think, cultural behavior that we just expect to leave messages for people in America.

272
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:31,330
And it's actually interesting because I've been to other countries where they also have voicemail messages and the service, but people are not as willing to actually leave the message.

273
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:33,560
So it happened to me.

274
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:38,490
For example, I call someone, I get their voicemail and I don't leave a message.

275
00:12:38,710 --> 00:12:39,230
I just hang up.

276
00:12:39,850 --> 00:12:40,250
And then they have...

277
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:41,840
You're bored or you're uncomfortable?

278
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:42,880
Why?

279
00:12:42,880 --> 00:12:43,880
I don't know.

280
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:48,590
Because, you know, if you have a mobile phone, it'll show up anyways that you have a missed call from this person.

281
00:12:49,300 --> 00:12:52,600
So then I really just, I don't really leave the message anymore.

282
00:12:52,600 --> 00:12:54,130
It's like, okay, you saw that I called you.

283
00:12:54,560 --> 00:12:58,860
Either I'll just call you back later or you'll call me back when you see the missed call.

284
00:13:00,400 --> 00:13:02,880
But yeah, for homes, obviously it's different.

285
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,800
You need to leave a message if you call someone.

286
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:07,840
Although sometimes it can be a bad thing.

287
00:13:07,900 --> 00:13:11,590
I definitely have a friend who loves to leave long messages.

288
00:13:12,190 --> 00:13:12,490
Oh, really?

289
00:13:12,920 --> 00:13:13,690
Hey, Catherine, it's me.

290
00:13:14,120 --> 00:13:19,480
So it's like Saturday at four o'clock and I was just thinking about calling and I was going to come over but I decided not to come over.

291
00:13:19,480 --> 00:13:24,550
But then I was thinking maybe I'll just call you instead and I can see that you're not home, but maybe you're out to dinner.

292
00:13:24,690 --> 00:13:25,370
I remember you saying...

293
00:13:25,370 --> 00:13:27,630
And they just go on and on and on.

294
00:13:28,130 --> 00:13:29,230
You actually, there is an end.

295
00:13:29,590 --> 00:13:31,550
They'll go beep and then it cuts you off.

296
00:13:31,600 --> 00:13:32,600
Right.

297
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:33,810
I think it's like 30 seconds or something like this.

298
00:13:34,600 --> 00:13:39,180
So it's very interesting, this whole voicemail thing, especially in the business world as well.

299
00:13:40,080 --> 00:13:43,560
Many times people go into a meeting, turn off their phones or put it on silent.

300
00:13:43,560 --> 00:13:46,680
And if you want to reach them or you want to leave a message, you've got to do the voicemail.

301
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:50,200
Yeah, calls will go right to voicemail so that you don't interrupt the meeting.

302
00:13:50,200 --> 00:13:51,200
Exactly.

303
00:13:51,200 --> 00:13:51,940
So that's all the time we have for today.

304
00:13:52,320 --> 00:13:53,880
Come to EnglishPod.com.

305
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:57,520
We more than welcome your questions, your comments, any suggestions.

306
00:13:57,520 --> 00:13:59,600
You can find us there, EnglishPod.com.

307
00:13:59,600 --> 00:13:59,800
See you.

308
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:01,600
Bye-bye.

309
00:14:01,600 --> 00:14:09,476
Have a great day.
